WEBVTT

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:04.445
How does a warehouse robot find the shortest route around two shelves?

00:00:04.445 --> 00:00:07.452
On this map, it starts at S and needs to reach G.

00:00:08.102 --> 00:00:09.487
Each move costs one.

00:00:09.487 --> 00:00:12.395
The robot can go right, down, left, or up.

00:00:12.395 --> 00:00:16.135
The dark squares are shelves, so it cannot enter them.

00:00:16.785 --> 00:00:19.578
Breadth-first search checks the nearest squares first.

00:00:19.578 --> 00:00:23.715
Watch the numbers appear in the order the workflow takes squares from its queue.

00:00:24.365 --> 00:00:26.675
Each square remembers where it came from.

00:00:26.675 --> 00:00:32.758
Starting at the goal and following those links back gives us this route: three steps right, then three down.

00:00:33.408 --> 00:00:35.581
Six moves is the best possible here.

00:00:35.581 --> 00:00:38.659
The goal is three columns over and three rows down.

00:00:38.659 --> 00:00:41.616
Our robot reaches it without a single extra step.

00:00:42.266 --> 00:00:44.766
Now put a shelf at each exit from the start.

00:00:44.766 --> 00:00:47.040
The queue empties after just one square.

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:50.450
This time the workflow reports that the goal is unreachable.

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:54.582
To try another layout, import the workflow into n8n.

00:00:54.582 --> 00:00:58.935
Edit the obstacles in Generate Warehouse Grid, then run it again.

